3 Spreadsheet I/O Usage
***********************

For spreadsheet I/O use is made of "interfaces"; an interface is just
terminology for a connection to software installed on the computer.  An
exception is the (default) ‘'OCT'’ interface which comprises just Octave
language and functions and as such needs no external SW. *Note Invoking
external spreadsheet I/O SW: XREFextsw, below for more details.  For an
extensive overview of supported interfaces, see the help for the
‘xlsopen’ function.

3.1 Efficient Spreadsheet I/O
=============================

The functions ‘xlsread’ and ‘xlswrite’ are provided in the io package.
Note that for every individual read/write operation these functions must
read the entire spreadsheet file from disk and, in case of writing,
write it back as well.  Therefore including these functions in a for
loop will be unduly time-consuming, and so they are mostly useful for
occasionally reading/writing data from a spreadsheet.
When repetitively reading data from or writing to optionally multiple
different sheets and/or different cell ranges in one spreadsheet file,
the io package provides functions for (1) opening a spreadsheet file,
(2) extracting and adding data, and (3) closing the spreadsheet and
possibly writing a changed spreadsheet back to disk.  These functions
are:

   • ‘xlsopen’ - opens a spreadsheet file and returns a pointer that
     contains a connection to the spreadsheet file contents.

   • ‘xls2oct’ - using the pointer, extracts raw data from a specific
     sheet and/or a specific cell range.
     The function ‘parsecell’ can be used to separate numeric and text
     data into separate double and cellstr arrays, resp.

   • ‘oct2xls’ - using the pointer, adds data to a specific cell range
     and/or specific sheet.

   • ‘xlsclose’ - closes the connection to the file contents and if
     these have changed, writes it all back to disk.

Once a speadsheet file has been 'opened' and a pointer is returned,
operations for extracting and adding can be mixed at will.  When used
this way the spreadsheet _file_ is read just once (and written just
once) for possibly multiple data extraction and adding operations.  In
fact, ‘xlsread’ and ‘xlswrite’ in the io package are mere wrappers
around these functions.
It is even possible to have multiple spreadsheet files open
simultaneously, e.g., for transferring data, optionally after
processing, from one spreadsheet to another.

*Caution*: do not forget to invoke ‘xlsclose’ after finishing
spreadsheet I/O operations to avoid stale pointers, data and maybe even
external SW needlessly keeping RAM and scratch disk space occupied.

3.2 Exploring spreadsheet files
===============================

The function ‘xlsfinfo’ can be used for exploring the contents of
spreadsheet files, i.e., the names and types of individual sheets in the
file and occupied cell ranges in all sheets.

3.3 Invoking external spreadsheet I/O SW
========================================

Most external SW interfaces for spreadsheet I/O are Java based.  The
required Java .jar files (collections of Java methods) must be added to
the javaclasspath.  On Windows, this will happen automatically if these
files are put in a subdirectory 'Java\' in the user's profile.
The windows package contains an interface to the Windows ActiveX system
library, which can be used to connect to various installed SW. In the io
package only an interface to MS-Excel is provided ('‘COM’'); a similar
interface to LibreOffice Calc was contributed to the Octave project but
hasn't been integrated in the io package.
The io package provides the function ‘chk_spreadsheet_support’ that
automatically runs various checks for required external SW for the
interfaces supported by the io package and optionally can find and
connect the required SW (adding to the javaclasspath).  On Windows
systems, this function is run when the io package is loaded.  When
unloading the io package, this function will remove the connections to
external SW that it set up when loading the io package.  On *nix systems
(including MacOS) this is disabled because the location of the various
required Java SW is not sufficiently standardized.

3.4 Extra options, Compatibility and Gotchas
============================================

Below some features of spreadsheet I/O with the io package functions are
highlighted.
   • Occupied cell ranges.
     ‘xlsfinfo’ in the io package will also return the ranges of
     occupied cells in each sheet in the spreadsheet file.  For the COM
     interface that may be less reliable because Visual Basic often
     includes empty but formatted cells as well in the tally.  For some
     spreadsheet file types, ‘xlsfinfo’ can also return the types of
     sheets (data, graphics, ...).

     The numeric and string arrays (and the raw arrays) returned by
     xlsread (as 1st, 2nd and 3rd output argument, resp.)  will be
     stripped from empty outer rows and columns, even if an explicit
     cell range to read from was specified.  However this behavior can
     be changed by a dedicated field in an option structure that can be
     given as input argument to ‘xlsread’.  ‘xlsread’ can return
     occupied cell ranges within the user specified cell range as the
     last output argument.

   • Returning formulas rather than formula results.
     ‘xlsread’ and ‘xls2oct’ allow returning formulas (i.e., their text)
     rather than computed values in a cell.  That is controlled by a
     dedicated field in an option structure that can be given as input
     argument to these functions.  See ‘xls2oct’ for more details.

   • Dates.
     Spreadsheet programs usually store dates (or date-times) as a
     specially formatted text string.  However, in .xls files (Excel 97
     / BIFF8 and Excel 95 / BIFF5) dates are stored as as the number of
     days passed since Dec.  31, 1899 ("epoch"); dates from before that
     epoch are stored as text strings.  ‘xlsread’ and ‘xls2oct’ infer
     from the filename extension what to do to and will return dates as
     number of days passed since AD. To that end, the number 693960 will
     be added to dates read from .xls spreadsheets, and subtracted from
     datenums before writing them to .xls.  But this is something to be
     aware of when reading data from .xls files and writing them to .ods
     or .xlsx, or vice versa.

   • Active sheet.
     When reading from or writing to a spreadsheet file, ‘xlsread’
     (‘xlsopen’) and ‘xlswrite’ (‘xlsclose’) ignore the "active" sheet.
     That implies that ‘xlsread’ (‘xls2oct’) by default reads from the
     first (leftmost) sheet in the stack rather than from the active
     sheet.  Similarly, ‘xlswrite’ (‘xlsclose’) write by default to the
     first / leftmost sheet.  Specify the required sheet number or
     -better- sheet name to overwrite that automatic sheet selection.

   • Cells with errors.
     Spreadsheet cells containing errors (e.g., due to wrong formulas or
     references in the spreadsheet) that spreadsheet programs show as
     e.g., ‘#Value’ are usually returned as blank cells.  But not all
     such errors can be catched.  Spreadsheets often contain cached
     values; when cells pointed to by formulas or references contain
     invalid info, the cached values from previous evaluations by the
     spreadsheet can still be present, and these may be returned by
     ‘xlsread’ and ‘xls2oct’.

   • Retaining formatting of cells in when writing to file.
     When adding or changing data in a worksheet and writing to file,
     cell formatting will be retained as much as possible.  The one
     exception is with the OCT interface; that will wipe all cell
     formatting in changed worksheets.  This is a known limitation of
     the OCT interface.


